hiya my name is elizabeth i'm a medical  student and an artist living in london  and something i haven't shared before on  this channel is that  i also work full-time alongside my  medical degree when i first started  medicine i did have a part-time job this  was working in customer service at  selfridges  but since then things have kind of  snowballed and for the last two years  i've been averaging around  40 hours of paid and unpaid work  alongside university at the moment i  have three jobs i'm a member of ali  abdal's team  where i am his executive assistant but i  also project manage some parts of the  business and  i'm involved in a few things there i  also work as a welfare lead for my  university  i provide peer support to students when  they need it and also i  am a tutor so i help students who want  to get into medicine with their bmat  ucat and interview preparation  i want to share what the positive  learning points of working full-time  alongside medicine have been  for me and although i am definitely not  advocating for this  sort of a workload for any sort of  medical student  there are those of us who don't have a  choice so perhaps this video  might help us see the silver lining to  having to do so much at the same time  and if you are fortunate enough not to  need to work during your degree perhaps  these learning points will help you  without actually having to do the work  because you know sharing is caring  so let's get straight into it the first  and perhaps the most important thing to  me  was around how i manage my time if  you've ever heard about parkinson's law  it basically states that any sort of  project will  fill the space that you allocate to it  so for example if i have an essay to  write and i say  i'm going to write this essay in the  next two weeks or i'm going to write  this essay  this afternoon i kind of found that the  quality of  my work was not very different in the  two cases the thing that i do now is  that i set  very very strict even ridiculous rules  for the things that i need to accomplish  for example i'll say  i'm going to learn dermatology tomorrow  and  although of course i won't be able to  cover everything i will cover  a lot more than i initially thought was  possible and then i'll just have small  things that i need to add to it later i  set myself  very rigid very narrow schedules for  tasks that i need  to do and this is somehow how i get a  lot of the things that i need to do  done otherwise i used to spend a lot of  the time dragging things out taking a  lot longer than was necessary  going down rabbit holes that i really  didn't need to explore spending time on  my phone  or just not being very efficient with  what i needed to do well if i know that  i need to complete some  massive project in two hours i will be  very very efficient with my time  the second thing that working alongside  medicine has taught me is a lot of  communication skills  i am so so grateful for the  communication training that we get in  university i think  kings is absolutely mind-blowingly  brilliant with this  however i've learned just as much if not  more  from my actual work especially working  in customer service or having to  troubleshoot things or having to pacify  people when they are very distressed  these real-life situations have happened  a lot more  in my actual work than they've happened  in medicine because obviously i'm a lot  junior and no one will let me handle  complicated  medical situations the practice that  i've gained from this kind of work is  absolutely invaluable  i found it has improved my confidence so  much more  and it's definitely translated to  medicine most of the time i'm  really comfortable around patients and i  love talking to them  and i think that if you are doing a  customer service job i would definitely  pay attention to those communication  skills  while you are at work too the next thing  that working full-time alongside  medicine has taught me  is how not to procrastinate when you  have a job something that you are paid  for and therefore will get fired if you  don't do properly  you don't have a choice anymore you have  to be at certain places at certain times  things need to be completed by their  deadlines they need to be done properly  there's no  case of i don't feel like doing this  right now or  i'm really not in the mood even though i  definitely still get those feelings i  have to get dressed and show up to work  for example and this is definitely  something that has  translated into my academic life too i  don't really give myself the option to  do things  i will put them in my calendar and then  i will absolutely follow through and do  them  i will treat my academic learning and  revision the same way that i treat my  job  it's something that i need to do it's  not for discussion so therefore i  procrastinate a lot less  because if the majority of the time i am  having to do things anyway  adding a few medical things onto that  too does not make a huge difference to  me  the next thing and i think this is the  absolutely most important for me  is how empowering having all these other  jobs has been there are definitely times  where i feel that medicine  is just not right and is not what i want  to do for the rest of my life  and in those times i always know that i  have a fallback option  i can always be a product manager or i  can be a writer or i can be an artist  and i know that it's unlikely that i  will be stuck  doing something i don't want to do  forever and  this is extremely liberating on the flip  side it also means that i know that for  as long as i stay in medicine it is  because i really really want to  and i enjoy doing it so knowing this for  me means that any time that i spend  around my patients or  doing things in medicine i value it a  lot more than i think i would have  otherwise the last thing i want to  mention is connections with people  outside of medicine  i am extremely guilty in the sense that  my closest friends are all doctors  however i think things would have been a  lot worse had i not had other jobs i  have met  some amazing people who have opened my  eyes to a world outside of medicine and  i know it sounds crazy but  being a medical student very often means  that your whole life  revolves around the hospital and  everything that goes on in there  i'm definitely one of those people who  very easily gets caught up in that  purely hospital  life so i found it very helpful to have  people who showed me that  youtube for example is a very fun thing  initially my plan for this video was to  talk about how to get  a part-time job and afford working  alongside a medical degree and i thought  under the current climate  that might be quite insensitive and  pointless so it's probably something  that i'm going to save for later  as always if you made it so far i do  hope that you got some sort of value  from this video  thank you so much for spending this time  with me i hope you have a wonderful rest  of your day  be kind to yourself and others and  believe everything you think thanks  [Music]  bye
 

